[Chapter "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D30"]
[Diagram "4...Be7"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]

{White: Develop with Nc3, Nf3 and Bg5, pinning the knight that guards d5. After an exchange on d5, push b4–b5 to create weaknesses on Black's queenside: the minority attack.

Black: Hold d5 with ...e6, ...Nf6 and ...Be7, then castle. Free your game with ...c5 or ...dxc4. Your problem piece is the c8 bishop, shut in behind e6: bring it out with ...b6 and ...Bb7, or after ...dxc4 and ...e5.}
1. d4 {Takes the center. The queen already guards d4.} d5 {Black claims an equal share of the center.}
2. c4 {The Queen's Gambit: White offers the c-pawn to lure Black's d-pawn away from the center.} e6 {This is the Queen's Gambit Declined. The e-pawn holds d5 but shuts in the c8 bishop.}
3. Nc3 {More pressure on d5.} Nf6 {Defends d5 again.}
4. Bg5 {Pins the knight that defends d5.} Be7 {Breaks the pin, ready to castle.} *

[Chapter "Tarrasch Defense"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense"]
[ECO "D32"]
[Diagram "4...exd5"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]

{White: Aim at the d5-pawn, which is often left isolated after ...cxd4. Blockade it on d4, press on it with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and your rooks, and trade minor pieces, because endgames favor the side playing against an isolated pawn.

Black: Accept an isolated d-pawn in return for free, active pieces: ...Nc6, ...Nf6, ...Be7 and castling. Use the space and the open lines to attack, and push ...d4 when it opens the game for you.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 {More pressure on d5.} c5 {This is the Tarrasch Defense. Black hits d4 at once, ready to accept an isolated d-pawn for active pieces.}
4. cxd5 {White's most common reply.} exd5 {The d5-pawn often ends up isolated once the c-pawns are traded.} *

[Chapter "Slav Defense"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D10"]
[Diagram "4...Bf5"]

{White: Develop with Nc3 and Nf3 and aim for e4. Once Black's bishop leaves c8, cxd5 and Qb3 hit the b7-pawn it no longer guards. If Black takes on c4, win the pawn back with a4 and e3.

Black: Support d5 with the c-pawn, so the c8 bishop stays free. Bring it out to f5 or g4 before you play ...e6. Then build a solid c6–d5–e6 triangle and castle.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {This is the Slav Defense. The c-pawn defends d5 and leaves the c8 bishop free.}
3. Nc3 {More popular than 3.Nf3 at club level.} Nf6 {Defends d5 and develops.}
4. Nf3 {The Three Knights Variation.} Bf5 {The bishop comes out before ...e6 can shut it in.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D43"]
[Diagram "4...c6"]

{White: Pin with Bg5, which allows the sharp Botvinnik and Moscow lines, or play the quieter e3 and Bd3. Aim for e4, and if Black takes on c4, win the pawn back with a lead in development.

Black: Build the c6–d5–e6 wall, then take on c4 once White's bishop has moved, so you gain a tempo. Expand with ...b5 and ...Bb7, then break with ...c5. Holding the c4-pawn after 5.Bg5 dxc4 leads to razor-sharp play.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 {Black declines the gambit.} 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c6 {This is the Semi-Slav: the e6-pawn of the Queen's Gambit Declined plus the c6-pawn of the Slav. Black now threatens to take on c4 and hold the pawn with ...b5.} *

[Chapter "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[ECO "D20"]
[Diagram "4...e5"]

{White: Take the center with e4 while the c4-pawn waits, then win it back with Bxc4. Use your lead in development and your center before Black catches up.

Black: Take on c4 to develop freely, but don't try to keep the pawn with ...b5: it usually costs too much. Hit back at White's center at once with ...e5 or ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 {This is the Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black gives up the center for a moment.}
3. Nc3 {The most common move at club level. White prepares e4.} Nf6 {Develops and watches e4.}
4. e4 {White takes the whole center.} e5 {Black strikes back at once. 4...e6 is played more often, but it leaves White slightly better.} *

[Chapter "Albin Countergambit"]
[Course "queens-gambit"]
[Group "1...d5: the classical replies"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Albin Countergambit"]
[ECO "D08"]
[Diagram "4...Nc6"]

{White: Keep the extra pawn and develop with Nf3, g3, Bg2, Nbd2 and castling. Don't play e3 early: 4.e3? Bb4+ 5.Bd2 dxe3 walks into the Lasker Trap. Later, round up the d4-pawn.

Black: The d4-pawn cramps White, so develop fast with ...Nc6, ...Be6 or ...Bg4, ...Qd7 and often castling long. Win back e5 with ...Nge7–g6 when you can. Stockfish rates the gambit about half a pawn worse for Black, so it works best as a surprise.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e5 {This is the Albin Countergambit. Black offers the e-pawn to break up White's center.}
3. dxe5 d4 {The d-pawn advances and cramps White: it controls c3 and e3.}
4. Nf3 {Develops and guards e5.} Nc6 {Attacks e5 and supports d4.} *

[Chapter "King's Indian Defense"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian defenses"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E61"]
[Diagram "4...d6"]

{White: Build a big center with c4, d4 and e4, develop calmly with Nf3 and Be2, and castle. When the center closes with d5, attack on the queenside with c5, b4 and the c-file.

Black: Let White have the center, fianchetto your bishop and castle. Then strike at the center with ...e5 or ...c5. Once the center is closed, attack the king with ...f5, ...f4 and ...g5: the game becomes a race.}
1. d4 Nf6 {Stops e4 and keeps Black's options open: the start of the Indian defenses.}
2. c4 g6 {Prepares to fianchetto the bishop.}
3. Nc3 Bg7 {This is the King's Indian Defense. The bishop will press on the long diagonal.}
4. e4 {White takes the whole center.} d6 {Stops e5 and prepares ...e5 or ...c5.} *

[Chapter "Nimzo-Indian Defense"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian defenses"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E20"]
[Diagram "4...O-O"]

{White: 4.Qc2 lets you take back on c3 with the queen, so you avoid doubled pawns. Then a3 wins the bishop pair and e4 takes the center. Your two bishops pay off once the position opens.

Black: Pin the c3 knight so White can't play e4. Give up the bishop for the knight when it costs White time or doubles the c-pawns. Strike back with ...d5 or ...c5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 {Prepares ...d5 or ...Bb4.}
3. Nc3 {Prepares e4.} Bb4 {This is the Nimzo-Indian Defense. The pinned knight can't support e4.}
4. Qc2 {The Classical Variation. If Black takes on c3, the queen takes back and White's pawns stay whole.} O-O {Black castles and keeps ...d5, ...c5 and ...Bxc3 in reserve.} *

[Chapter "Grünfeld Defense"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian defenses"]
[Opening "Grünfeld Defense"]
[ECO "D80"]
[Diagram "4...Nxd5"]

{White: Take the whole center with e4, usually after 5.e4 Nxc3 6.bxc3, then back it up with Nf3, Be3 or Bc4. A center that holds cramps Black and supports an attack.

Black: Let White build the center, then attack it with ...Bg7, ...c5 and ...Nc6. Pile up on d4 with your pieces, and open lines with ...cxd4. Trade pieces once the center is weak.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 {This is the Grünfeld Defense. Black challenges the center at once.}
4. cxd5 {The Exchange Variation, White's most popular reply.} Nxd5 {After 5.e4 the knight is traded on c3, and White gets the big center Black wants to attack.} *

[Chapter "Queen's Indian Defense"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian defenses"]
[Opening "Queen's Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E12"]
[Diagram "4...Bb7"]

{White: Avoid the Nimzo-Indian pin with 3.Nf3, then fianchetto with g3 and Bg2 to contest the long diagonal. Castle, then aim for e4 or d5 when Black's grip loosens.

Black: Control e4 and d5 from a distance with ...b6 and ...Bb7. Develop calmly with ...Be7 and castling, then break with ...c5 or ...d5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 {Keeps the knight off c3, so ...Bb4 pins nothing.} b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Defense. The bishop will go to b7.}
4. g3 {White fianchettoes too, to fight for the long diagonal.} Bb7 {Watches e4 and d5.} *

[Chapter "Benoni Defense and Benko Gambit"]
[Group "1...Nf6: the Indian defenses"]
[Opening "Benoni Defense"]
[ECO "A56"]
[Diagram "4...exd5"]
[Lines "Modern Benoni | Benko Gambit"]

{White: Take space with d5 and e4. Against the Benoni, push e4–e5 to break through in the center. Against the Benko, keep the extra pawn, find a safe home for your king and use your central space.

Black: In the Benoni, play ...exd5, ...d6, ...g6 and ...Bg7, then advance your queenside majority with ...a6 and ...b5. In the Benko, give a pawn for open a- and b-files that press on White's queenside for the rest of the game.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 {Black hits d4 from the side.}
3. d5 {White gains space instead of trading.} e6 {This is the Modern Benoni. Black will open the e-file.}
(3... b5 {This is the Benko Gambit. Black offers a queenside pawn.} 4. cxb5 a6 {After 5.bxa6 Bxa6, White stays a pawn up, but Black's rooks get the open a- and b-files.})
4. Nc3 exd5 {After 5.cxd5, White has a central pawn majority and Black a queenside one.} *

[Chapter "Dutch Defense"]
[Group "Other replies for Black"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense"]
[ECO "A80"]
[Diagram "4...Be7"]

{White: Fight for e4 with Nc3 and Bg5, which pins a defender of e4. Aim at the light squares that ...f5 loosened, such as e6 and the e8–h5 diagonal.

Black: Claim e4 with ...f5 and ...Nf6, then pick a setup: Classical (...e6, ...Be7, ...d6), Stonewall (...e6, ...d5) or Leningrad (...g6). Castle, then attack on the kingside with ...Qe8–h5 or ...f4. Keep an eye on your own king, which ...f5 weakened.}
1. d4 f5 {This is the Dutch Defense. The f-pawn controls e4.}
2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 {Fights for e4.} e6 {The Classical setup: ...Be7 and castling follow.}
4. Bg5 {Pins the knight that guards e4.} Be7 {Breaks the pin.} *

[Chapter "Budapest Gambit"]
[Group "Other replies for Black"]
[Opening "Indian Defense: Budapest Gambit"]
[ECO "A51"]
[Diagram "4...Nc6"]

{White: Hold e5 for a while with Nf3 and Bf4, but keep developing; giving the pawn back calmly is fine. Watch the trap 5.Bf4 Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 Qe7 7.a3 Ngxe5 8.axb4?? Nd3#.

Black: Win back e5 with ...Nc6, ...Bb4+ and ...Qe7, developing quickly as you go. Stockfish rates the gambit about half a pawn worse for Black, so it works best as a surprise.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e5 {This is the Budapest Gambit. Black offers the e-pawn at once.}
3. dxe5 Ng4 {The knight hits e5 straight away.}
4. Nf3 {The Adler Variation. The knight guards e5.} Nc6 {Attacks e5 a second time.} *

[Chapter "London System"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Diagram "4...c5"]

{White: Play the same setup almost every game: d4, Bf4, e3, Nf3, c3, Nbd2, Bd3 and castling. The c3–d4–e3 pawn triangle is hard to break. Later, Ne5 and a kingside attack are typical.

Black: Hit d4 with ...c5 and ...Nc6, and use ...Qb6 against the b2-pawn the f4 bishop left behind. Trade off White's good bishop with ...Bd6, or chase it with ...Nh5.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 {This is the London System. The bishop comes out before e3 can shut it in.} Nf6
3. e3 {Builds the pawn triangle.} e6
4. Nf3 c5 {The most active answer: Black hits d4 at once.} *

[Chapter "Colle System"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Colle System"]
[ECO "D04"]
[Diagram "4...c5"]

{White: Build the same setup every game: d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3, Nbd2, c3 and castling. Then break with e3–e4, which opens lines toward Black's king for the d3 bishop.

Black: Develop with ...c5, ...Nc6, ...Bd6 and castling, and meet e4 by trading in the center. Bringing the c8 bishop out to f5 or g4 before ...e6 (the Anti-Colle) blunts White's d3 bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e3 {This is the Colle System. White builds a solid center first.} e6 {Black's most common reply. It shuts in the c8 bishop, so 3...Bf5 and 3...Bg4 are popular too.}
4. Bd3 {The bishop aims at h7 and supports e4.} c5 {Black hits d4 at once.} *

[Chapter "Trompowsky Attack"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Trompowsky Attack"]
[ECO "A45"]
[Diagram "4...Nd5"]

{White: 2.Bg5 threatens Bxf6 to double Black's pawns, and leaves the usual 1.d4 theory behind. If Black allows it, take on f6; if not, use e4 and e5 to gain space with tempo.

Black: Avoid cheap doubled pawns with ...Ne4, ...e6 or ...d5. Here ...Be7 breaks the pin; after 5.Bxe7 Qxe7, hit back at the center with ...d6 or ...c5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 {This is the Trompowsky Attack. White threatens Bxf6.} e6 {The Classical Defense: if Bxf6, the queen takes back and the pawns stay whole.}
3. e4 {The Big Center Variation.} Be7 {Breaks the pin. 3...h6 is the other main move.}
4. e5 Nd5 {The knight finds a central square. Next, 5.Bxe7 Qxe7.} *

[Chapter "Torre Attack"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Torre Attack: Classical Defense"]
[ECO "A46"]
[Diagram "4...d5"]

{White: Pin the f6 knight with Bg5, then build with e3, Nbd2 and c3 and play for e4. A quiet system that avoids most theory and keeps a solid center.

Black: Break the pin with ...Be7, or ask the bishop with ...h6. Then claim the center with ...d5 and ...c5. Once ...c5 is in, Black is comfortable.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bg5 {This is the Torre Attack. The bishop pins the knight to the queen before White plays c4 or e3.} Be7 {Breaks the pin, as in the Queen's Gambit Declined.}
4. e3 {Supports d4 and opens the f1 bishop's diagonal.} d5 {Black takes a share of the center.} *

[Chapter "Catalan Opening"]
[Group "White's systems"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening"]
[ECO "E00"]
[Diagram "4...Be7"]

{White: Fianchetto with g3 and Bg2, so the bishop presses along the long diagonal against b7 and Black's queenside. If Black takes on c4, win the pawn back with Qc2, Qa4+ or Ne5. Steady pressure at little risk.

Black: Stay solid with ...Be7, ...O-O and ...c6, or take on c4 and try to keep the pawn with ...a6 and ...b5. Free your c8 bishop with ...dxc4 and ...c5, or with ...b6 and ...Bb7.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 {This is the Catalan Opening. The bishop will go to g2.} d5
4. Bg2 {The bishop aims at d5 and b7.} Be7 {Black develops and castles first. Taking on c4 can come later.} *
